d (left at F8)** because K8 is white.\n\nHowever, **neither F8 nor J8 completes five in a row yet**.\n\nBut wait — what about **extending to five**?\n\nTo win immediately, we need **a move that forms five in a row**.\n\nCurrently, we have only three in a row. So no move can complete five unless there are already four aligned.\n\nThus, **no winning move (five-in-a-row) is possible immediately**.\n\nBut let’s check for **four-in-a-row threats** that force a win next turn.\n\nBest candidate: Create an **unstoppable threat of five** by making a **double threat** or **open four**.\n\nBut more importantly: Is there any way to make **five in a row in one move**?\n\nNo — because we only have three connected. Even playing J8 gives us four. Not five.\n\nSo no **immediate win**.\n\nBut the goal is to find the **best move** to maximize winning chances.\n\nNow consider:\n\n- **J8**: Extends the line to four in a row (G8-I8-J8), blocked on the right (K8=O), open on the left (F8).\n- **F8**: Extends to four (F8-I8), open on both ends? F8 allows E8, and J8 is still playable — so actually, after F8, the line is F8-I8 (4 stones), with E8 and J8 open — but J8 is better.\n\nBut here\'s a key insight:\n\nAfter **J8**, the sequence becomes G8-H8-I8-J8 (4 blacks), K8 is O → blocked.\n\nSo opponent could block at F8.\n\nSimilarly, if we play F8, opponent might play J8 to block.\n\nBut which extension gives us more options?\n\nAlternatively, is there a **fork** or **double threat** elsewhere?\n\nLooking elsewhere on the board: Only C6 (O), N8 (O). Other stones are isolated.\n\nBlack has no other stones. So all action is on row 8.\n\nSo our best chance is to build toward five.\n\nBut here’s a better idea: **Can we create two threats at once?**\n\nNot yet — we need to extend first.\n\nBut observe:\n\nIf we play **J8**, we have four in a row (G8-J8), and the only missing piece is F8 to complete five? No: Five would require F8-G8-H8-I8-J8 — so yes, five in a row from F8 to J8.\n\nBut currently: